  <sys:String x:Key="Text.RemovesSupersededVersionsOfWindowsComponents" xml:space="preserve">Removes superseded versions of Windows components.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.WindowsWillRemoveSupersededComponentVersionsItNo" xml:space="preserve">Windows will remove superseded component versions it no longer needs. This is maintenance, not a repair of damaged files. It may take several minutes; no specific amount of recovered space is promised. Requires administrator privileges. The computer will not restart automatically. This version does not use /ResetBase or any additional cleanup options. Once started, it is allowed to finish without forced cancellation.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.ReadOnlyFileSystemCheckOf" xml:space="preserve">Read-only file system check of </sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.TheDriveContainingTheCurrentWindowsInstallationThis" xml:space="preserve">, the drive containing the current Windows installation. This read-only check may take some time. It does not repair, schedule boot-time checks, change the dirty bit, restart or shut down the computer. Checking an active partition without locking it may report inconsistencies requiring later verification; a warning alone does not establish permanent damage. Requires administrator privileges. Once started, it is allowed to finish without forced cancellation.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.InternalCleanupOfKnownCategories" xml:space="preserve">Internal cleanup of known categories</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.NoArbitraryPathsCommandsOrPatterns" xml:space="preserve">No arbitrary paths, commands or patterns</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.CleanWindowsTemporaryFiles" xml:space="preserve">Clean Windows temporary files</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.TheRootFolderIsRetainedDatesAndAttributes" xml:space="preserve">
The root folder is retained. Dates and attributes are revalidated immediately before deletion. Junctions, symbolic links and reparse points are not followed. Files in use, locked or inaccessible are skipped; permissions are not changed and processes are not terminated. Only eligible empty folders are deleted. The same folder is then reanalyzed within the same worker, without a second UAC request. Requires administrator privileges; cancellation is not forced.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.InternalNetCleanupOfWindowsTemporaryFiles" xml:space="preserve">Internal .NET cleanup of Windows temporary files</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.InternallyResolvedPathFilesInUseOrProtected" xml:space="preserve">Internally resolved path; files in use or protected files are skipped; followed by read-only reanalysis</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.AnalyzeWindowsTemporaryFiles" xml:space="preserve">Analyze Windows temporary files</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.ReadOnlyAnalysisWithAdministratorPrivileges" xml:space="preserve">Read-only analysis with administrator privileges.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.CalculatesAccessibleSizeFileCountAndPotentiallyCleanable" xml:space="preserve">
Calculates accessible size, file count and potentially cleanable size for eligible files. No files or folders are deleted, permissions are not changed and reparse points are not followed. Inaccessible items are skipped and a partial result is reported. The estimate does not guarantee recoverable space. Requests a single UAC elevation for the same executable; the main application remains unelevated.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.InternalNetAnalysisOfWindowsTemporaryFilesRead" xml:space="preserve">Internal .NET analysis of Windows temporary files (read-only)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.NoExternalProcessesOrPathsSuppliedByThe" xml:space="preserve">No external processes or paths supplied by the interface</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.ResetsTcpIpStackParametersAndMayChange" xml:space="preserve">Resets TCP/IP stack parameters and may change network configuration.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.MakesRealChangesToWindowsTcpIpAnd" xml:space="preserve">Makes real changes to Windows TCP/IP and DHCP parameters and may change network configuration, including existing IP addresses and other settings. May interrupt connectivity, including RDP sessions. Windows will usually need to be restarted to complete the reset. The application never restarts automatically. Requires administrator privileges and requests one UAC elevation after confirmation. Not combined with Winsock or another tool. Partial failures are retained and explained; they are not corrected automatically. Cancellation is not forced, and the operation must finish before the application closes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.ResetsTheWinsockCatalogUsedByApplicationsTo" xml:space="preserve">Resets the Winsock catalog used by applications to communicate over the network.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.MakesRealChangesResetsTheWinsockCatalogTo" xml:space="preserve">Makes real changes: resets the Winsock catalog to a clean state and may remove custom LSP providers. May temporarily affect applications and connectivity, including remote sessions. Does not reset TCP/IP or manually change IP or DNS servers. Windows may need to be restarted to complete the change. The application never restarts automatically. Requires administrator privileges; requests one UAC elevation after confirmation. The operation is not forcibly cancelled and must finish before the application closes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.DisablesAndReEnablesAnActivePhysicalEthernet" xml:space="preserve">Disables and re-enables an active physical Ethernet or Wi-Fi adapter.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.ConnectivityOnTheSelectedAdapterWillBeTemporarily" xml:space="preserve">Connectivity on the selected adapter will be temporarily lost, including RDP sessions. Requires administrator privileges. Does not manually change IP or DNS; does not reset Winsock or TCP/IP. Reconnection may renegotiate the link and DHCP. Restarting continues only if Windows confirms the disabled state. If enable fails, it is attempted up to three times and manual recovery instructions are provided. Does not restart the computer; the application cannot be closed and cancellation is not forced during the operation.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.RequiresAnInternalSelectionOfAnActivePhysical" xml:space="preserve">(requires an internal selection of an active physical adapter)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.NoCommandWithoutSelectionAndConfirmation" xml:space="preserve">(no command without selection and confirmation)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.ReleasesAndRenewsIpv4AddressesOnActivePhysical" xml:space="preserve">Releases and renews IPv4 addresses on active physical interfaces configured through DHCP.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.ConnectivityMayBeTemporarilyLostIncludingRemoteSessions" xml:space="preserve">Connectivity may be temporarily lost, including remote sessions. Does not flush the DNS cache or manually change DNS; does not reset Winsock or TCP/IP configuration. DHCP may assign different addresses, gateways or DNS servers. First attempted without elevation; only an explicit permission denial requests UAC for the same confirmed selection. If release fails or cannot be confirmed, renew is skipped for that interface. The computer is not restarted and cancellation is not forced.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.RequiresAnInternalSelectionOfDhcpInterfaces" xml:space="preserve">(requires an internal selection of DHCP interfaces)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.RemovesDnsResponsesTemporarilyCachedByWindows" xml:space="preserve">Removes DNS responses temporarily cached by Windows.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.FlushesTheLocalDnsResolutionCacheIncludingNegative" xml:space="preserve">Flushes the local DNS resolution cache, including negative responses. Subsequent queries can retrieve fresh responses. Does not change DNS servers, IP, gateway or DHCP; does not restart adapters, clear browser caches or guarantee an Internet fix. First runs with normal privileges. Only an explicit Windows permission denial requests UAC for this same fixed action, after confirmation. No restart required. This brief operation is allowed to finish without forced cancellation.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.FullRepair" xml:space="preserve">Full repair</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.ScanhealthRestorehealthOnlyForRepairableCorruptionSfc" xml:space="preserve">ScanHealth → RestoreHealth only for repairable corruption → SFC.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.1AnalyzeWindowsImageScanhealth2RepairImage" xml:space="preserve">1. Analyze Windows image — ScanHealth.
2. Repair image if corruption is detected — RestoreHealth (conditional).
3. Check system files — SFC.
If ScanHealth returns Healthy, RestoreHealth is skipped. If it returns RepairRequired, repair runs and SFC continues only if RestoreHealth returns Repaired or Healthy. Failure, an unknown result or cancellation stops the sequence and explains skipped steps. RestoreHealth and SFC make real changes. May take several minutes. Requests a single UAC elevation; does not restart automatically and cancellation is not forced.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.OnlyIfScanhealthRepairrequired" xml:space="preserve">[Only if ScanHealth = RepairRequired]
</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.AnalyzeWindowsImage" xml:space="preserve">Analyze Windows image</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.RepairWindowsImage" xml:space="preserve">Repair Windows image</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.InDepthComponentStoreAnalysisWithoutRepair" xml:space="preserve">In-depth component store analysis without repair.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.ChecksAndRepairsProtectedSystemFiles" xml:space="preserve">Checks and repairs protected system files.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.AnalyzesAndRepairsTheComponentStore" xml:space="preserve">Analyzes and repairs the component store.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.DismScanhealthDoesNotAttemptRepairItIs" xml:space="preserve">DISM ScanHealth does not attempt repair. It is more thorough than CheckHealth and may detect corruption not yet recorded.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.SfcChecksAllProtectedFilesAndAutomaticallyAttempts" xml:space="preserve">SFC checks all protected files and automatically attempts to repair damaged files. Makes real changes. Microsoft recommends DISM RestoreHealth before SFC when addressing corruption.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.DismRestorehealthAnalyzesAndAutomaticallyRepairsCorrectableCorruption" xml:space="preserve">DISM RestoreHealth analyzes and automatically repairs correctable corruption. Makes real changes and normally uses Windows Update to obtain required components. /Source and /LimitAccess are not supplied. Does not restart automatically.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.RequiresAdministratorPrivilegesAndMayTakeSeveralMinutes" xml:space="preserve"> Requires administrator privileges and may take several minutes. Once started, it is allowed to finish without forced cancellation.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.QuicklyChecksWhetherWindowsHasAlreadyRecordedComponent" xml:space="preserve">Quickly checks whether Windows has already recorded component store corruption.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Text.DismCheckhealthQueriesTheRecordedStateOfThe" xml:space="preserve">DISM CheckHealth queries the recorded state of the component store; it does not attempt repair or perform an exhaustive scan. Requires administrator privileges and is normally quick. Depending on the result, ScanHealth or RestoreHealth may be recommended; neither runs automatically. /English only makes output consistent; it does not change the Windows language. Once started, it is allowed to finish without forced cancellation.</sys:String>
